===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# To [[reap]] grain, [[gather]] it up, and [[store]] it in a [[granary]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1920|author=Edward Carpenter|authorlink=Edward Carpenter|title=Pagan and Christian Creeds|year_published=1921|location=New York|publisher=Harcourt, Brace and Co.|page=195|passage=The rustic does not know what would happen to him if he '''garnered''' his corn on Sunday, nor does the diner-out in polite society know what would happen if he spooned up his food with his knife - but they both are stricken with a sort of paralysis at the very suggestion of infringing these taboos.}}
# To [[gather]], [[amass]], [[hoard]], as if harvesting grain.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1835|author=[[w:Honoré_de_Balzac|Honoré de Balzac]]|title=[[w:Le Lys dans la vallée|The Lily of the Valley]]|section=[[s:The Lily of the Valley (Balzac, tr. Wormeley)/Chapter II|Chapter 2]]
|passage={{...|To find a flower in its native haunts}} I walked enormous distances{{...|, beside the brooklets, through the valleys, to the summit of the cliffs, across the moorland,}} '''garnering''' thoughts even from the heather.}}
#* '''1913''', [[s:Catholic Encyclopedia (1913)/Anton Berlage|“Anton Berlage”]] in ''Catholic Encyclopedia, 1913''
#*: He '''garnered''' the fruit of his studies in seven volumes.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1956|author=[[w:Andre_Norton|Andrew North]]|title=[[w:Plague_Ship|Plague Ship]]|section=[[s:Plague_Ship/Chapter_XIV|Chapter 14]]|passage={{...|If a school of them were to be found in the vicinity no wonder this village was now deserted as}} its fleet went out to '''garner''' in the elusive but highly succulent fish.}}
# {{lb|en|often|_|figurative}} To [[earn]]; to [[get]]; to [[accumulate]] or [[acquire]] by some effort or due to some fact
#: {{syn|en|reap|gain}}
#: {{co|en|to '''garner''' support}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''garnered''' a reputation as a language expert.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her new book '''garnered''' high praise from the critics.}}
#: {{ux|en|His poor choices '''garnered''' him a steady stream of welfare checks.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1983|author=[[w:Ronald_Reagan|Ronald Reagan]]|title=[[s:Proclamation_5031|Proclamation 5031]]
|passage=This country will never forget nor fail to honor those who have so courageously '''garnered''' our highest regard.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=[[w:Bill_Clinton|Bill Clinton]]|title=[[s:Proclamation_7259|Proclamation 7259]]|passage=President Roosevelt '''garnered''' the support of our working men and women to increase war production and build our "Arsenal of Democracy."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Adrian Horton|title=Netflix lists AI job worth $900,000 amid twin Hollywood strikes|work=The Guardian|date=2023-07-26|url=https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/26/netflix-ai-job-hollywood-strikes|issn=0261-3077|passage=The AMPTP has disputed that description as a “mischaracterization” of their proposal meant to “'''garner''' support for its work stoppage.”}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} To gather or become gathered; to accumulate or become accumulated; to become stored.
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|pages=9–10|volume=III|text=She soothed herself with the belief that the workings of her soul were still known to him,—that her regret and her despondency were but the needful preparation for that other sphere, where now her only remaining hope was '''garnered.'''}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|canto=LXXXII|passage=For this alone on Death I wreak / The wrath that '''garners''' in my heart;}}

====Usage notes====
The "earn, acquire, accumulate" sense should be read as a figurative extension of the original "harvest, gather" sense, sometimes with some inanimate achievement or choice metaphorically doing the "gathering", as "''The new book garnered high praise''", or with an indirect object, as, "''The new book garnered <u>the author</u> high praise''". In this sense, the achievement, choice, or fact is actively gathering something, positive or negative, for its creator, even if that choice is inaction, as in "''Failure to try can garner you the disapproval of the industrious''".

